{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 837 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "5451e5e959fc6fd0c314c46accf3ef6efc451cc2801a143ebd76a72f863f31b2" :edb/db-before-t 836 :edb/db-after-t 837 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534149 50 #inst "2026-10-11T17:14:03.058Z" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186151837 1071 "What is $26 \\textrm{ mod } 3?$" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186151837 1071 "What is $26 \\textrm{mod} 3$?" 13194139534149 17592186045417 false] [17592186151837 1074 "The residue of $26$ modulo $3$ is the integer\n\n$$\n0 \\leq r < 3\n$$\n\nsuch that\n\n$$\n26 \\equiv r \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,3)\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r$, we need to write $26$ as an integer multiple of $3$, plus the residue $r$.\n\nWe note that $26 \\equiv 2 \\, (\\textrm{mod}\\,3)$ because\n\n$$\n26 = 8\\cdot 3 + {\\color{blue}{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $26$ modulo $3$ is ${\\color{blue}{2}}$.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the subtraction property. Remember that any integer multiple of $3$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $3$.\n\n$$\n26 \\mid ≡26 - 0\\begin{vmatrix}(mod3) \\\\ ≡26 - 8 \\cdot 3\\end{vmatrix}(mod3); ≡26 - 24\\begin{vmatrix}(mod3) \\\\ ≡2\\end{vmatrix}(mod3)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $26$ modulo $3$ is ${\\color{blue}{2}}$." 13194139534149 17592186045417 false] [17592186151837 1074 "The residue of $26$ modulo $3$ is the integer $0 \\leq r <  3$ such that\n\n$$\n26 \\equiv r \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,3).\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r,$ we need to write $26$ as an integer multiple of $3,$ plus the residue $r.$\n\nWe note that $26 \\equiv 2 \\, (\\textrm{mod}\\,3)$ because\n\n$$\n26 = 8\\cdot 3 + {\\color{blue}{2}} .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $26$ modulo $3$ is ${\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the subtraction property. Remember that any integer multiple of $3$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}26 & ≡26-0 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\  & ≡26-8\\cdot 3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\  & ≡26-24 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\  & ≡2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $26$ modulo $3$ is ${\\color{blue}{2}}.$" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186151838 1071 "What is $-13 \\textrm{ mod } 4?$" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186151838 1071 "What is $-13 \\textrm{mod} 4$?" 13194139534149 17592186045417 false] [17592186151838 1074 "The residue of $-13$ modulo $4$ is the integer\n\n$$\n0 \\leq r < 4\n$$\n\nsuch that\n\n$$\n-13 \\equiv r \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,4)\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r$, we need to write $-13$ as an integer multiple of $4$, plus the residue $r$.\n\nWe note that $-13 \\equiv 3 \\, (\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$ because\n\n$$\n-13 = (-4)\\cdot 4 + {\\color{blue}{3}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-13$ modulo $4$ is ${\\color{blue}{3}}$.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the addition property. Remember that any integer multiple of $4$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $4$.\n\n$$\n-13 \\mid ≡ - 13 + 0\\begin{vmatrix}(mod4) \\\\ ≡ - 13 + 4 \\cdot 4\\end{vmatrix}(mod4); ≡ - 13 + 16\\begin{vmatrix}(mod4) \\\\ ≡3\\end{vmatrix}(mod4)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-13$ modulo $4$ is ${\\color{blue}{3}}$." 13194139534149 17592186045417 false] [17592186151838 1074 "The residue of $-13$ modulo $4$ is the integer $0 \\leq r < 4$ such that\n\n$$\n-13 \\equiv r \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,4).\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r,$ we need to write $-13$ as an integer multiple of $4,$ plus the residue $r.$\n\nWe note that $-13 \\equiv 3 \\, (\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$ because\n\n$$\n-13 = (-4)\\cdot 4 + {\\color{blue}{3}} .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-13$ modulo $4$ is ${\\color{blue}{3}}.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the addition property. Remember that any integer multiple of $4$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-13 & ≡-13+0 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡-13+4\\cdot 4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡-13+16 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-13$ modulo $4$ is ${\\color{blue}{3}}.$" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186151857 1071 "Let $a$ be a positive integer. Construct an expression that gives the last digit of $24a+15$." 13194139534149 17592186045417 false] [17592186151857 1071 "Let $a$ be a positive integer. Construct an expression that gives the last digit of $24a+15.$" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186151857 1074 "Let\n\n$$\nb=24a+15\n$$\n\nNote that $b$ is positive. Now, let $d_3$, $d_2$, and $d_1$ be the last three digits of the number $b{:}$\n\n$$\nb = \\ldots d_3 d_2 d_1\n$$\n\nThen, $b$ can be written in expanded form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb &= ⋯ + 100d_{3} + 10d_{2} + d_{1} \\\\\n&= 10(⋯ + 10d_{3} + d_{2}) + d_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the residue of $b$ modulo $10{:}$\n\n$$\nb \\mid ≡10(⋯ + 10d_{3} + d_{2}) + d_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}(mod10) \\\\ ≡0 \\cdot (⋯ + 10d_{3} + d_{2}) + d_{1}\\end{vmatrix}(mod10); ≡0 + d_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}(mod10) \\\\ ≡d_{1}\\end{vmatrix}(mod10)\n$$\n\nNotice that $d_1$ is the last digit of $b$.\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\nb = 24a+15\n$$\n\nand\n$24\\equiv 4, \\qquad 15\\equiv 5 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,10)$\nwe have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}b & ≡d_{1} & (mod10) \\\\ 24a + 15 & ≡d_{1} & (mod10) \\\\ 4a + 5 & ≡d_{1} & (mod10)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the expression that gives the last digit of $24a+15$ is the following:\n\n$$\n(4a+5) \\: \\textrm{mod}\\: 10\n$$" 13194139534149 17592186045417 false] [17592186151857 1074 "Let $b=24a+15.$ Note that $b$ is positive. Now, let $d_3,$ $d_2,$ and $d_1$ be the last three digits of the number $b{:}$ \n\n$$\nb = \\ldots d_3 d_2 d_1\n$$\n\nThen, $b$ can be written in expanded form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =⋯+100{d}_{3}+10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} \\\\  & =10(⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the residue of $b$ modulo $10{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ≡10(⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0\\cdot (⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $d_1$ is the last digit of $b.$\n\nFinally, since $b = 24a+15,$ and \n\n$$\n24\\equiv 4, \\qquad 15\\equiv 5 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,10)\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ 24a+15 & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ 4a+5 & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression that gives the last digit of $24a+15$ is the following:\n\n$$\n(4a+5) \\: \\textrm{mod}\\: 10\n$$" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186152055 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412370 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186152055 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412375 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186152055 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412378 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186152055 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412380 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186152055 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412382 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186152056 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412371 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186152056 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412372 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186152056 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412376 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186152056 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412377 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186152056 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412379 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186152057 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412373 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186152057 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412374 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186152057 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412381 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186152057 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412383 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186152370 1050 420.0 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186152476 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149405 13194139534149 17592186045417 false] [17592186152476 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149410 13194139534149 17592186045417 false] [17592186152476 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412356 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186152476 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412357 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186152476 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186152476 1071 "Let $a$ be a positive integer. Construct an expression that gives the last digit of $a$.$\\div$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}" 13194139534149 17592186045417 false] [17592186152476 1071 "Let $a$ be a positive integer. Construct an expression that gives the last digit of $a.$" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186152476 1074 "Let ${d}_{3},$ ${d}_{2},$ and ${d}_{1}$ be the last three digits of the number $a:$ \n\n$$\na=…{d}_{3}{d}_{2}{d}_{1}\n$$\n\nThen, $a$ can be written in expanded form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⋯+100{d}_{3}+10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} \\\\  & =10(⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the residue of $a$ modulo $10:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & ≡10(⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0\\cdot (⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${d}_{1}$ is the last digit of $a.$\n\nTherefore, the expression that gives the last digit of $a$ is the following:\n\n$$\na\\,\\text{mod}\\,10\n$$" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186153448 1064 "The **residue** of an integer $a$ modulo $n$ is the *unique* integer\n\n$$\n0 \\leq r < n\n$$\n\nsuch that\n\n$$\na \\equiv r \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nThis integer $r$ is sometimes called the **least residue** of $a$ modulo $n$.\n\nFor example, consider the following statements:\n\n$$\n25\\begin{vmatrix}≡1 & (mod6) \\\\ 25 & ≡7\\end{vmatrix}(mod6); 25\\begin{vmatrix}≡13 & (mod6) \\\\ 25 & ≡19\\end{vmatrix}(mod6); ⋮\n$$\n\nAll of these statements are true. However, we say that \"the residue of $25$ modulo $6$ is ${\\color{blue}{1}}$\", because\n\n$$\n0\\leq {\\color{blue}{1}} < 6\n$$\n\nWe write this as\n\n$$\n25 \\textrm{mod} 6 = 1\n$$\n\nNotice that there are no parentheses around the $\\textrm{mod}$. When there are no parentheses, it means that we're treating $\\textrm{mod}$ as though it were a type of function. This function takes our integer $25$ as its input and returns the remainder that we get when $25$ is divided by $6$.\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7mod6 &= 1 \\\\\n13mod6 &= 1 \\\\\n19mod6 &= 1 \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe'll present two methods of computing residues. Make sure that you understand *both* methods." 13194139534149 17592186045417 false] [17592186153448 1064 "The **residue** of an integer $a$ modulo $n$ is the *unique* integer $0 \\leq r <  n$ such that\n\n$$\na \\equiv r \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nThis integer $r$ is sometimes called the **least residue** of $a$ modulo $n.$ \n\nFor example, consider the following statements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25 & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ 25 & ≡7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ 25 & ≡13\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ 25 & ≡19\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll of these statements are true. However, we say that \"the residue of $25$ modulo $6$ is ${\\color{blue}{1}}$\", because $0\\leq {\\color{blue}{1}} < 6.$ We write this as\n\n$$\n25 \\textrm{ mod } 6 = 1.\n$$\n\nNotice that there are no parentheses around the $\\textrm{mod}$. When there are no parentheses, it means that we're treating $\\textrm{mod}$ as though it were a type of function. This function takes our integer $25$ as its input and returns the remainder that we get when $25$ is divided by $6.$ \n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\text{ mod }6 & =1 \\\\ 13\\text{ mod }6 & =1 \\\\ 19\\text{ mod }6 & =1 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe'll present two methods of computing residues. Make sure that you understand *both* methods." 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186153464 1064 "Some interesting problems in number theory require finding the last few digits of a large integer. We can use properties of congruences to help us solve these types of problems.\n\nConsider a positive number $a$, where the digit $d_1$ is in the ones place, $d_2$ is in the tens place, $d_3$ is in the hundreds place, and so on.\n\n$$\na = \\ldots d_3 d_2 d_1\n$$\n\nThen, $a$ can be written in expanded form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= ⋯ + 100d_{3} + 10d_{2} + d_{1} \\\\\n&= 10(⋯ + 10d_{3} + d_{2}) + d_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the residue of $a$ modulo $10{:}$\n\n$$\na \\mid ≡10(⋯ + 10d_{3} + d_{2}) + d_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}(mod10) \\\\ ≡0 \\cdot (⋯ + 10d_{3} + d_{2}) + d_{1}\\end{vmatrix}(mod10); ≡0 + d_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}(mod10) \\\\ ≡d_{1}\\end{vmatrix}(mod10)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression $a \\: \\textrm{mod}\\: 10$ returns the last digit of $a$.\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n25\\,409\\,19{\\color{blue}3} \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: 10 = {\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nUsing similar reasoning,\n\n- the expression $a \\: \\textrm{mod}\\: 100$ returns the last two digits of $a$, e.g.,\n$25\\,409\\,1{\\color{blue}93} \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: 100 = {\\color{blue}93}$.\n- the expression $a \\: \\textrm{mod}\\: 1\\,000$ returns the last three digits of $a$, e.g.,\n$25\\,409\\,{\\color{blue}193} \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: 1\\,000 = {\\color{blue}193}$,\n\nand so on." 13194139534149 17592186045417 false] [17592186153464 1064 "Some interesting problems in number theory require finding the last few digits of a large integer. We can use properties of congruences to help us solve these types of problems.  \n\nConsider a positive number $a,$ where the digit $d_1$ is in the ones place, $d_2$ is in the tens place, $d_3$ is in the hundreds place, and so on.\n\n$$\na = \\ldots d_3 d_2 d_1\n$$\n\nThen, $a$ can be written in expanded form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⋯+100{d}_{3}+10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} \\\\  & =10(⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n