2})+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${d}_{1}$ is the last digit of $b.$\n\nFinally, since $b=12a+11,$ and \n\n$$\n12≡2,\\,11≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ 12a+11 & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ 2a+1 & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression that gives the last digit of $12a+11$ is the following:\n\n$$\n2a+1\\,\\text{mod}\\,10\n$$" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412374 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412354 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412374 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412359 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412374 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412369 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412374 1069 "q-213325" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412374 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412374 1071 "Let $a$ be a positive integer. Construct an expression that gives a two-digit number consisting of the last two digits of $215a.$" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412374 1072 #uuid "39ab88df-75d5-5304-96dd-f494bc2a5583" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412374 1074 "Let $b=215a.$ Note that $b$ is positive. Now, let ${d}_{4},$ ${d}_{3},$ ${d}_{2},$ and ${d}_{1}$ be the last four digits of the number $a:$ \n\n$$\na=…{d}_{4}{d}_{3}{d}_{2}{d}_{1}\n$$\n\nThen, $a$ can be written in expanded form as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⋯+1000{d}_{4}+100{d}_{3}+10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} \\\\  & =100(⋯+10{d}_{4}+{d}_{3})+10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the residue of $a$ modulo $100:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & ≡100(⋯+10{d}_{4}+{d}_{3})+10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,100) \\\\  & ≡0\\cdot (⋯+10{d}_{4}+{d}_{3})+10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,100) \\\\  & ≡0+10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,100) \\\\  & ≡10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,100)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1}$ is the two-digit number consisting of the last two digits of $a.$\n\nFinally, since $b=215a,$ and \n\n$$\n215≡15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,100)\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ≡10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,100) \\\\ 215a & ≡10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,100) \\\\ 15a & ≡10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,100)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression that gives the last digit of $215a$ is the following:\n\n$$\n15a\\,\\text{mod}\\,100\n$$" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412362 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412375 1069 "q-102405" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412375 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412375 1071 "$39\\text{ mod }4=$" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412375 1072 #uuid "44155916-7397-5b39-a575-6db603bf8071" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412375 1074 "The residue of $39$ modulo $4$ is the integer $0\\le r<4$ such that\n\n$$\n39≡r\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4).\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r,$ we need to write $39$ as an integer multiple of $4,$ plus the residue $r.$\n\nWe note that $39≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$ because\n\n$$\n39=9\\cdot 4+3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $39$ modulo $4$ is $3.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the subtraction property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}39 & ≡39-0 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡39-9\\cdot 4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡39-36 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $39$ modulo $4$ is $3.$" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412349 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412376 1069 "q-76960" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412376 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412376 1071 "$-9\\text{ mod }4=$" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412376 1072 #uuid "549f3a8c-907a-5b2d-8f07-7849e3e239ab" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412376 1074 "The residue of $-9$ modulo $4$ is the integer $0\\le r<4$ such that\n\n$$\n-9≡r\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4).\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r,$ we need to write $-9$ as an integer multiple of $4,$ plus the residue $r.$\n\nWe note that $-9≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$ because\n\n$$\n-9=(-3)\\cdot 4+3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-9$ modulo $4$ is $3.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the addition property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9 & ≡-9+0 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡-9+3\\cdot 4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡-9+12 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-9$ modulo $4$ is $3.$" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412377 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412368 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412377 1069 "q-213076" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412377 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412377 1071 "$-7\\text{ mod }2=${{field-1}}" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412377 1072 #uuid "6d645eda-2214-5faa-a63b-2fee859e4238" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412377 1074 "The residue of $-7$ modulo $2$ is the integer $0\\le r<2$ such that\n\n$$\n-7≡r\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2).\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r,$ we need to write $-7$ as an integer multiple of $2,$ plus the residue $r.$\n\nWe note that $-7≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)$ because\n\n$$\n-7=(-4)\\cdot 2+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-7$ modulo $2$ is $1.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the addition property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7 & ≡-7+0 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,2) \\\\  & ≡-7+4\\cdot 2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,2) \\\\  & ≡-7+8 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,2) \\\\  & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-7$ modulo $2$ is $1.$" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412378 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412347 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412378 1069 "q-213046" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412378 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412378 1071 "$27\\text{ mod }4=${{field-1}}" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412378 1072 #uuid "7119d9e9-8c81-54dd-a944-533c5aa1aed1" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412378 1074 "The residue of $27$ modulo $4$ is the integer $0\\le r<4$ such that\n\n$$\n27≡r\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4).\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r,$ we need to write $27$ as an integer multiple of $4,$ plus the residue $r.$\n\nWe note that $27≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$ because\n\n$$\n27=6\\cdot 4+3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $27$ modulo $4$ is $3.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the subtraction property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27 & ≡27-0 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡27-6\\cdot 4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡27-24 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $27$ modulo $4$ is $3.$" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412379 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412352 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412379 1069 "q-76976" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412379 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412379 1071 "$-49\\text{ mod }7=$" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412379 1072 #uuid "9feebe3a-396b-5d25-94bd-022707c998e2" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412379 1074 "The residue of $-49$ modulo $7$ is the integer $0\\le r<7$ such that\n\n$$\n-49≡r\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r,$ we need to write $-49$ as an integer multiple of $7,$ plus the residue $r.$\n\nWe note that $-49≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$ because\n\n$$\n-49=(-7)\\cdot 7+0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-49$ modulo $7$ is $0.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the addition property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-49 & ≡-49+0 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡-49+7\\cdot 7 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡-49+49 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡0 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-49$ modulo $7$ is $0.$" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412380 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412358 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412380 1069 "q-76947" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412380 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412380 1071 "$52\\text{ mod }5=$" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412380 1072 #uuid "ab21706d-8653-5637-8475-77432c4bfb1b" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412380 1074 "The residue of $52$ modulo $5$ is the integer $0\\le r<5$ such that\n\n$$\n52≡r\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5).\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r,$ we need to write $52$ as an integer multiple of $5,$ plus the residue $r.$\n\nWe note that $52≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$ because\n\n$$\n52=10\\cdot 5+2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $52$ modulo $5$ is $2.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the subtraction property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}52 & ≡52-0 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡52-10\\cdot 5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡52-50 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $52$ modulo $5$ is $2.$" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412381 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412350 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412381 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412355 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412381 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412360 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412381 1069 "q-213090" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412381 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412381 1071 "Let $a$ be a positive integer. Construct an expression that gives the last digit of $15a.$" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412381 1072 #uuid "c3a0e9ee-2041-5135-bc63-3a864333d5da" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412381 1074 "Let $b=15a.$ Note that $b$ is positive. Now, let ${d}_{3},$ ${d}_{2},$ and ${d}_{1}$ be the last three digits of the number $b:$ \n\n$$\nb=…{d}_{3}{d}_{2}{d}_{1}\n$$\n\nThen, $b$ can be written in expanded form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =⋯+100{d}_{3}+10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} \\\\  & =10(⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the residue of $b$ modulo $10:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ≡10(⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0\\cdot (⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${d}_{1}$ is the last digit of $b.$\n\nFinally, since $b=15a,$ and \n\n$$\n15≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ 15a & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ 5a & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression that gives the last digit of $15a$ is the following:\n\n$$\n5a\\,\\text{mod}\\,10\n$$" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412382 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412365 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412382 1069 "q-213027" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412382 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412382 1071 "$14\\text{ mod }6=${{field-1}}" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412382 1072 #uuid "da9aaccf-ed58-5942-8a55-f387c4ff158b" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412382 1074 "The residue of $14$ modulo $6$ is the integer $0\\le r<6$ such that\n\n$$\n14≡r\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6).\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r,$ we need to write $14$ as an integer multiple of $6,$ plus the residue $r.$\n\nWe note that $14≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$ because\n\n$$\n14=2\\cdot 6+2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $14$ modulo $6$ is $2.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the subtraction property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14 & ≡14-0 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡14-2\\cdot 6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡14-12 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $14$ modulo $6$ is $2.$" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412383 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412346 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412383 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412348 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412383 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412364 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412383 1069 "q-213093" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412383 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412383 1071 "Let $a$ be a positive integer. Construct an expression that gives the last digit of $16a-13.$" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412383 1072 #uuid "e4503f6b-114f-5eeb-b12d-95fc38bf1562" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true] [17592186412383 1074 "Let $b=16a-13.$ Note that $b$ is positive. Now, let ${d}_{3},$ ${d}_{2},$ and ${d}_{1}$ be the last three digits of the number $b:$ \n\n$$\nb=…{d}_{3}{d}_{2}{d}_{1}\n$$\n\nThen, $b$ can be written in expanded form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =⋯+100{d}_{3}+10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} \\\\  & =10(⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the residue of $b$ modulo $10:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ≡10(⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0\\cdot (⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${d}_{1}$ is the last digit of $b.$\n\nFinally, since $b=16a-13,$ and \n\n$$\n16≡6,\\,-13≡-3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ 16a-13 & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ 6a-3 & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression that gives the last digit of $16a-13$ is the following:\n\n$$\n6a-3\\,\\text{mod}\\,10\n$$" 13194139534149 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0015065c-e020-549d-8843-ecb2b21a3462" 17592186412259 "answer-0179ab06-b666-5897-8b46-ee9334267d51" 17592186412260 "answer-025335b4-a543-5316-babf-b941123d0854" 17592186412261 "answer-02c566de-7afa-5c5b-9076-1b5c4c862420" 17592186412262 "answer-04db3f48-6af3-50ad-b665-4fb2472e5c3a" 17592186412263 "answer-0542fa10-23f6-572f-9b84-a34a655e4e13" 17592186412264 "answer-0655b49a-db5a-59a7-be19-739cf6bc3c60" 17592186412265 "answer-080ba340-0c2e-55b9-adbc-0e8561a2fd34" 17592186412266 "answer-093000e5-24a5-5cd8-98e2-b485b5430a35" 17592186412267 "answer-0e06888c-a058-5a34-9559-91f136c9f661" 17592186412268 "answer-0fe8e591-ad12-52f4-a2f8-1bca3dfd6296" 17592186412269 "answer-13f843d4-6653-57d0-9b3c-9c37ec913b8b" 17592186412270 "answer-1532bdde-984c-58ae-9a0a-62a3cb85c963" 17592186412271 "answer-17acc15b-5128-5c4a-a66a-3f4a98cc058d" 17592186412272 "answer-1915435a-1ef7-53cf-b21e-3e4a383dab4a" 17592186412273 "answer-1fb6b485-00ae-55b8-895e-218fb3282422" 17592186412274 "answer-21aa2954-a8d1-507a-b84b-e5072dbc08e8" 17592186412275 "answer-21cbee92-d821-599b-aac4-a6279372c248" 17592186412276 "answer-22a7612f-73dd-54d0-af2d-7674b3f15667" 17592186412277 "answer-23664f96-879c-51bd-89f0-dede9cc02253" 17592186412278 "answer-25faa3bd-9411-5f2e-a2b7-564638b4ee31" 17592186412279 "answer-2afe2d65-4054-594e-b4e8-a303a39cda0d" 17592186412280 "answer-2e60a1c1-361a-59b7-a94d-06e92eeefa00" 17592186412281 "answer-3339da0a-421f-5923-8220-0d40e5fd564c" 17592186412282 "answer-379ee39b-848a-531f-87be-6adf177ef1a8" 17592186412283 "answer-3c268c10-167f-5a67-8207-c306dbc438ea" 17592186412284 "answer-3d69ecac-a052-597a-990f-d18d2a8b1970" 17592186412285 "answer-3e435929-e6f5-5c22-a0c6-a1cc0a33abed" 17592186412286 "answer-3f7a2e7d-a7f7-51a9-b5d1-e6a08a235d60" 17592186412287 "answer-3fff7f15-6f97-5291-9721-ed269a080c33" 17592186412288 "answer-429fdcde-3b6d-5c73-8dc3-bcb